body:
The 8 MB failure in the last comment looks like zstd's window limit rather than a file-size limit. "Frame requires too much memory for decoding" is libzstd's `ZSTD_error_frameParameter_windowTooLarge`, which fires when a frame declares a `Window_Size` larger than the decoder allows. Capping the decoder at `windowLogMax = 23` (8 MiB, the limit RFC 9659 sets for `Content-Encoding: zstd`) reproduces that message exactly. An 11 MiB file compressed with an 8 MiB window still decodes fine under the same cap:

| Frame | Declared window | zstd 1.5.7 CLI, Node 26.13.1 `node:zlib`, Bun 1.4.4 `DecompressionStream("zstd")` | `node:zlib` with `windowLogMax = 23` |
|---|---|---|---|
| 11 MiB, `zstd -19` | 8 MiB | ok | ok |
| 11 MiB, `zstd --ultra -22` | 11 MiB (single segment) | ok | Frame requires too much memory for decoding |
| 201 MiB, `zstd -3 --long=27` | 128 MiB | ok | Frame requires too much memory for decoding |
| 201 MiB, `zstd -3 --long=28` | 201 MiB (single segment) | fails in all 3 | fails |

So a large file fails only when its encoder declared a large window. `--ultra -22` on an 11 MiB input writes a single-segment frame whose window is the whole 11 MiB, and `--long=27` declares 128 MiB. Bun's own `CompressionStream("zstd")` writes 2 MiB windows for both inputs, so its output decodes under either limit.

That means implementations already disagree on which `"zstd"` streams they accept. Bun 1.4.4 decodes windows up to 128 MiB, which is libzstd's default (`ZSTD_WINDOWLOG_LIMIT_DEFAULT` is 27). Firefox's decoder behind the pref looks like it caps at 8 MiB: same message, same threshold, though I haven't run Firefox myself. RFC 9659 §3 settled this for HTTP by making 8 MB a MUST for decoders and encoders, but it only covers the content coding.

If zstd goes into this spec, I think it needs its own number. RFC 9659's 8 MiB keeps memory bounded and matches what browsers already enforce for HTTP. libzstd's 128 MiB default covers the 200 MB files above. A WPT test at the limit and one just past it would keep implementations from drifting. Once there's a number, I'm happy to write those tests.

<details><summary>Repro: zstd 1.5.7, Node 26.13.1, Bun 1.4.4, macOS 27.3 arm64</summary>

The inputs are seeded word lists, 11 MiB and 201 MiB, so they're very compressible. Only the declared window matters here.

```bash
zstd -19 in11.txt -o a.zst                  # Window Size: 8.00 MiB
zstd --ultra -22 in11.txt -o b.zst          # Window Size: 11.0 MiB, single segment
zstd -3 --long=27 in200.txt -o c.zst        # Window Size: 128 MiB
zstd -3 --long=28 in200.txt -o d.zst        # Window Size: 201 MiB, single segment
zstd -lv b.zst | grep "Window Size"
```

```js
// node dec.mjs <file> [windowLogMax]
import { readFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { zstdDecompressSync, constants } from "node:zlib";
const [file, cap] = process.argv.slice(2);
const opts = cap ? { params: { [constants.ZSTD_d_windowLogMax]: Number(cap) } } : {};
try { console.log("ok", zstdDecompressSync(readFileSync(file), opts).length); }
catch (e) { console.log("FAIL", e.code, e.message); }
```

```js
// bun dec.ts <file>
const body = Bun.file(process.argv[2]).stream().pipeThrough(new DecompressionStream("zstd"));
try { console.log("ok", (await new Response(body).arrayBuffer()).byteLength); }
catch (e) { console.log("FAIL", e.message); }
```

`node dec.mjs b.zst 23` prints `FAIL ZSTD_error_frameParameter_windowTooLarge Frame requires too much memory for decoding`. Without the cap, and in Bun, it prints `ok 11534336`.

</details>
